Azure Services Required 
Azure Hybrid Identity with Password Hash Synchronisation 
1. Microsoft Azure 
Microsoft Azure provides the cloud environment in which the hybrid identity laboratory is hosted. The Azure 
subscription is used to provision the virtual machine and supporting networking resources required for the project. 
For this implementation, only the resources necessary for the identity lab should be deployed to control cost and 
resource usage. 
2. Azure Virtual Network (VNet) 
An Azure Virtual Network provides the private network environment for the Windows Server virtual machine. The 
VNet and its subnet allow the server to communicate with required Azure and identity services while providing a 
controlled network boundary for the laboratory environment. Network security rules can be configured where 
required to restrict unnecessary inbound and outbound traffic. 
3. Azure Virtual Machine 
An Azure Virtual Machine running Windows Server is used to host the on-premises identity components required for 
the demonstration. Active Directory Domain Services and Microsoft Entra Connect can be installed on this server for 
the lab. In a production environment, these roles would normally be designed with additional servers and separation 
according to Microsoft's recommended architecture; the combined setup is used here as a controlled academic 
demonstration. 
4. Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) 
Active Directory Domain Services provides the on-premises identity directory. It stores the project's test users, groups 
and organizational units and acts as the source directory for synchronization. Although AD DS is a Windows Server 
role rather than an Azure service, it is a core component of the hybrid architecture and therefore must be included in 
the required technology stack. 
5. Microsoft Entra ID 
Microsoft Entra ID is the cloud identity and access management service used by the project. It receives synchronized 
identities from the on-premises Active Directory environment and provides cloud authentication and access control 
for supported applications and Azure resources. 
6. Microsoft Entra Connect 
Microsoft Entra Connect provides the synchronization mechanism between Active Directory and Microsoft Entra ID. It 
is installed on the Windows Server and is configured to synchronize selected users, groups and identity attributes. The 
project uses it to demonstrate hybrid identity synchronization and Password Hash Synchronisation. 
7. Password Hash Synchronisation (PHS) 
Password Hash Synchronisation is the selected authentication method for this project. It enables password-derived 
information from on-premises Active Directory to be synchronized to Microsoft Entra ID. The original plaintext 
password is not synchronized. This allows users to use the same password for supported cloud authentication 
scenarios. 
8. Azure Resource Group 
The Azure Resource Group provides a logical container for the Azure resources used by the project. The virtual 
machine, virtual network and related resources can be managed together, making it easier to monitor, configure and 
remove the complete laboratory environment when the project is finished. 
9. Azure Storage (Optional) 
Azure Storage is not required for the core Password Hash Synchronisation workflow. It may be used optionally for 
storing project-related logs, exported configuration information or other non-sensitive project artifacts. It should not 
be introduced unless the implementation requires it. 
10. Azure Monitor / Activity Logs (Optional) 
Azure Monitor and Azure Activity Logs can optionally be used to observe Azure resource activity and assist with 
infrastructure-level troubleshooting. They are supporting services rather than core requirements for the PHS 
demonstration. 
